  "primer": {
    "text": "The United States and China are locked in strategic competition, a broad contest for economic, technological and security influence often framed as growth versus rivalry, where cooperation and rivalry run together. Summitry between the American and Chinese leaders is the main way to set direction, send alignment signals to allies and rivals, and keep crisis channels open so incidents do not escalate. In this context, strategic competition means managing rivalry without direct conflict; crisis channels, including a proposed AI hotline, are dedicated lines to defuse fast-moving military or technological incidents; panda diplomacy is the symbolic exchange of pandas used to signal goodwill or a thaw.",
    "built_at": "2026-10-04T07:26:06.472952+00:00"
  },
  "current": {
    "date": "2026-10-05",
    "bottom_line": "No new US-China policy moves since the Sept 23-25 Washington summit; the picture is still a holding truce to Jan 10, 2027 with symbolism outweighing substance. Parallel signals — Taiwan opening a new office in Arizona Oct 3-5 and PLA troops drilling with Russia at Tsentr-2026 through Oct 3 — show alignment competition continuing around the summit. Confidence is high the truce holds near-term, lower that rare-earth flows or AI safeguards will be operational before the November Shenzhen and December Miami meetings.",
