  "timeline": [
    {
      "date": "2026-10-04",
      "headline": "Rare-earth magnet flows to US still depressed; summit leaves no documented deal",
      "detail": "Chinese customs data for August 2026 showed rare-earth magnet exports to US fell to 512 tons, down 21% month-on-month and 13% year-on-year, while total magnet exports slipped 18% year-on-year, arriving days before summit. At summit, no jointly documented rare-earth settlement; White House said only sides 'continue to work' to return shipments to 'appropriate levels,' China's statement silent on rare earths. China controls an estimated 90% of global rare-earth processing and magnet fabrication (~88% light oxide separation, 97% heavy), US holds ~15,000 tonnes annual separation capacity vs ~45,000 tonnes demand; rebuilding full mine-to-magnet chain could take up to 15 years per GAO.",
      "where": "",
      "verification": "researched",
      "sources": [
        "https://investornews.com/critical-minerals-rare-earths/technology-metals-report-10-04-2026-a-summit-without-a-rare-earth-deal-leaves-the-west-buying-its-way-to-supply",
        "https://bisi.org.uk/reports/chinas-rare-earth-processing-dominance-and-what-the-trump-xi-summit-didnt-change"
      ],
      "from": "/geopolitics/2026-10-05"
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-03",
      "headline": "Taiwan opens de facto consulate in Phoenix days after summit",
      "detail": "Lin arrived in Phoenix late Oct 3 for three-day visit Oct 3-5 to open the Taipei Economic and Cultural Office (TECO) in Phoenix, Taiwan's 14th representative office in the US. Program includes meetings with Hobbs and Gallego, Taiwanese investors, and an MOU between Arizona Commerce Authority and Taiwan's National Innovation and Entrepreneurship Association; Larson flew from Washington to accompany Lin. No Washington stop or State Department meetings, per sources. Office is framed around TSMC's Arizona semiconductor corridor — $165 billion committed for six fabs, two packaging facilities and R&D on ~900-2000 acre north Phoenix campus, with an additional $100 billion announced in 2026 raising total to $265 billion, described as largest foreign direct investment in US history.",
      "where": "Phoenix, Arizona, United States",
      "verification": "researched",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3369770/days-after-xi-trump-summit-top-taiwan-diplomat-opens-de-facto-consulate-us",
        "https://www.axios.com/local/phoenix/2026/07/27/taiwan-taipei-economic-cultural-office-phoenix-tsmc-semiconductors"
      ],
      "from": "/geopolitics/2026-10-05"
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-02",
      "headline": "Summit judged pageantry over substance; WWII-allies language unsettles Tokyo and Taipei",
      "detail": "Post-summit assessments Sept 30-Oct 2 split but converged on symbolism over substance: War on the Rocks experts called summit 'pageantry and procrastination,' Lowy Institute said Trump rolled out red carpet and handed Xi a narrative win, CFR called it high symbolism low substance with only pandas as clear winner. White House fact sheet Sept 25 said US and China 'were allies in World War II and fought side by side,' with Trump praising Flying Tigers and Xi recalling fighting 'side by side' against Japanese militarism. Lowy noted China immediately published Xi's full remarks while White House released only brief summary after Xi left, giving Xi narrative advantage. WWII-allies framing raised concerns in Tokyo about Sino-US rapprochement sidelining Japan and in Taipei about Beijing appropriating Republic of China's wartime legacy; Wu accused Beijing of 'narrative diplomacy.' Takaichi said Sept 27 Trump gave her 'detailed' phone briefing after Xi visit and both confirmed they are 'most mutually trusting allies.' Xi pressed Trump to move Taiwan language from 'not supporting' to 'opposing' independence; White House fact sheet did not mention Taiwan.",
      "where": "Washington, DC, United States",
      "verification": "researched",
      "sources": [
        "https://warontherocks.com/triumph-or-procrastination-experts-split-on-the-trump-xi-summit/",
        "https://www.lowyinstitute.org/the-interpreter/trump-rolled-out-the-red-carpet-and-handed-xi-a-narrative-win",
        "https://www.cfr.org/articles/the-limits-of-trump-and-xis-photo-op-summit",
        "https://thediplomat.com/2026/10/concerns-about-wwii-allies-at-us-china-summit/",
        "https://www.scmp.com/news/china/politics/article/3369692/taipeis-dilemma-over-history-deepens-xi-trump-praise-wwii-flying-tigers",
        "https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2026/09/27/japan/politics/us-japan-takaichi-trump-china"
      ],
      "from": "/geopolitics/2026-10-05"
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-02",
      "headline": "Chinese troops drill with Russia at Tsentr-2026 as summit truce holds",
      "detail": "Russia held strategic command-and-staff exercise Tsentr-2026 Sept 28-Oct 3 at Chebarkul training ground, Chelyabinsk Oblast, with more than 47,000 personnel from SCO countries; Putin observed at Chebarkul Oct 2. PLA troops deployed via road, rail and air fielded Type 99A tanks, drone swarms, tactical UAVs, armed helicopters and unmanned ground vehicles, practicing 3D reconnaissance, integrated information-fire strikes, obstacle breaching and multi-axis assaults; began withdrawing by air/rail Oct 3-4. Russian MoD said exercise applied lessons from war in Ukraine to 'repel external aggression in the Central Asian direction' with mass drones, ground robots, electronic warfare, Geran-4/5 drones, Zarya lasers and Izdeliye 347 missiles, with troops stationed underground to avoid drone strikes.",
      "where": "Chebarkul, Russia",
      "verification": "researched",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.scmp.com/news/china/military/article/3369657/chinese-troops-line-russias-tsentr-2026-exercise-military-ties-tighten",
        "https://www.globaltimes.cn/page/202610/1371666.shtml"
      ],
      "from": "/geopolitics/2026-10-05"
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-27",
      "headline": "Panda diplomacy renewed as pair arrives in Atlanta on 10-year loan",
      "detail": "At the White House Sept 25, Xi announced two giant pandas — Ping Ping (male) and Fu Shuang (female), born in 2020 at Chengdu Research Base — would arrive in Atlanta in a few days. They landed Sept 27 at Hartsfield-Jackson International Airport via FedEx Boeing 777 charter from Chengdu Shuangliu after a 16-hour flight, on a 10-year loan.",
      "where": "Atlanta, Georgia",
      "verification": "researched",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/27/giant-pandas-arrive-in-atlanta-as-part-of-10-year-us-china-agreement",
        "https://www.aa.com.tr/en/asia-pacific/explainer-how-panda-diplomacy-became-part-of-us-china-ties/4069307",
        "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/26/china-us-to-open-ai-communication-channel-after-summit-white-house-says"
